Day 6
Pentesting Fundamentals

(TryHackMe – Penetration Testing Basics)

🔍 Task 1: What is Penetration Testing?
📌 Overview

A Penetration Test (Pentest) is an authorised, ethical security assessment where testers simulate real attackers to identify vulnerabilities in systems, networks, or applications.

Pentesting uses:

The same tools

The same techniques

The same methodologies

that a real attacker would use — but with permission.

📊 On average, there is 1 cyber attack every 39 seconds.

🎯 Why Pentesting Matters

Cybersecurity affects everyone (individuals & organisations)

Helps protect:

Devices

Applications

Sensitive data

⚖️ Task 2: Penetration Testing Ethics
🧠 Legal vs Ethical Hacking

Pentesting is legal only when authorised by the system owner.
Anything outside the agreed scope = illegal.

Before testing starts:

Tester & organisation agree on:

Tools

Techniques

Targets
➡️ This forms the Scope.

🎩 Types of Hackers
Hat Type	Description	Example
White Hat	Legal & ethical	Authorised pentester
Grey Hat	Mixed ethics, may break laws	Taking down scam sites
Black Hat	Criminal intent	Ransomware attackers
📜 Rules of Engagement (ROE)

A formal document defining how the pentest is conducted.

Section	Purpose
Permission	Legal authorisation
Test Scope	What can/can’t be tested
Rules	Allowed & disallowed techniques
✅ Task 2 Answers

Hacker with permission → White Hat

Stealing data → Black Hat

Document defining engagement → Rules of Engagement

🧭 Task 3: Penetration Testing Methodologies

A methodology is the structured approach a pentester follows.
There is no one-size-fits-all, but most follow similar stages.

🔄 Common Pentesting Stages
Stage	Description
Information Gathering	OSINT, public info (no scanning)
Enumeration / Scanning	Discover services & apps
Exploitation	Exploit vulnerabilities
Privilege Escalation	Gain higher access
Post-Exploitation	Pivoting, data collection, cleanup, reporting
